WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.417
JENNY BROWN: In
our last video, we

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.750
talked about what makes the
Cloud Talent Solution Job

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:05.640
Search API a good
fit for improving

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.440
the quality of job searching.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.110
Now, let's look at the best
ways to implement the API

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:13.500
into your job search solution
and go over planning details

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.250
to get started.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:18.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:21.095
One important thing to note
about the Job Search API

00:00:21.095 --> 00:00:23.690
is that it's a search
index of your jobs.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.270
This means it's intended to
work alongside your existing

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.078
database by mirroring
the contents

00:00:28.078 --> 00:00:29.870
and searching against
it in a manner that's

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.240
optimized to find the most
relevant results to a search

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.330
query.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.330
This means it acts something
like a middle layer

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:37.040
for the underlying system.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.500
In order for the API to best
determine relevant search

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.860
results, it stores information
that you provide about the jobs

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:44.840
and uses a pretrained
machine learning model

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.200
with your provided data to
return relevant results.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.450
Once the API is integrated
into your system,

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:53.570
your users will see high-quality
search results immediately.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:56.000
For many businesses, this
improvement in search quality

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.778
is more than sufficient.

00:00:57.778 --> 00:00:59.570
If you want to further
refine the ordering,

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.360
the API also offers
configuration options

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.879
as well as the ability to
further train the model.

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:07.482
We'll talk about these
in depth in later videos.

00:01:07.482 --> 00:01:09.440
You'll want to make sure
that you keep your job

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.480
database as the
primary source of truth

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.780
and use the Job Search
API as a search index.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.400
With that in mind,
let's take a look

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.150
at how to plan out
an implementation

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:22.130
so you can connect your job
solution to the Job Search API.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:23.990
To get the data
into the API, you'll

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.660
connect it to your database,
but not to your front end

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:27.950
quite yet.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.780
Using the Google
Client Library, which

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.000
is available in a
number of languages,

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.490
you can access the Job
Search API directly

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.980
or you can implement a custom
connector through the REST

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.140
or RPC interface.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.030
Once connected, you
can start by sending

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.370
a list of jobs and
companies to the API, which

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.850
then analyzes and indexes
them into a searchable form.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:48.650
When uploading the
data to the API,

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:51.170
it's important to consider
your capacity needs.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.420
Capacity planning makes
sure the API can provide

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.970
the appropriate support for
all of the daily operations,

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.640
such as adding and deleting
jobs and companies.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.140
Here are some
factors to consider

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:01.400
when looking at capacity.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.430
How frequently are jobs
created, updated, and deleted?

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.699
How often do job
seekers receive alerts?

00:02:06.699 --> 00:02:08.449
For both of these,
you'll need to estimate

00:02:08.449 --> 00:02:10.460
the capacity for
batch data loads

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:12.500
as well as normal daily usage.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.900
How frequently are job
seekers doing searches?

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.290
Capacity here should be measured
for peak and daily usage.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.480
With capacity planning set
up you're in good shape

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.070
to start uploading data.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:24.500
Once your data is uploaded,
the Job Search API

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.268
will be ready to use, thanks
to the built-in ML models.

00:02:28.268 --> 00:02:29.810
When designing your
integration, it's

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:31.910
important to ensure that
your solution is built

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.620
in a fault-tolerant manner.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.750
That way, if the
Job Search API Index

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.380
needs to be tweaked
for any reason,

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.380
you can route traffic
back to your existing back

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.580
end without interrupting your
users' job search experience.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.140
We'll go through and share more
information and tips to help

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.490
you plan your implementation.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:48.140
Stay tuned for our
next video where

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.223
we'll dive a little deeper
and look at configuring

00:02:50.223 --> 00:02:51.717
search results from the API.

00:02:51.717 --> 00:02:52.550
Thanks for watching.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:54.310
And remember, when
looking for talent,

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.920
it's OK to keep your
head in the cloud.

